    "q-161775": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161775",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196753/assets/e63035cbe368a4f41b7197d70880d0639f004e26996fe6b5055e69966e0f66ee.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
